1
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:05,690
This is Energy Twin, a digital twin for existing hotels. See the savings before you spend.

2
00:00:05,790 --> 00:00:18,890
First-time users get a guided tour: a quick one in eight steps, or the full one.

3
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:21,620
Chapter one: a city-scale twin.

4
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:26,770
The homepage shows the whole portfolio: energy spend, savings potential and carbon across five Chicago hotels.

5
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:37,650
It tells the chain where to start. Not the biggest bill, but the best ten-year return per dollar invested.

6
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:47,320
Every hotel sits on a 3D map of Chicago, built from OpenStreetMap buildings, and colored by the metric you choose.

7
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:55,560
Click a hotel, and the camera flies in among the towers.

8
00:00:55,660 --> 00:01:02,130
Optional data columns raise each hotel's metric above the city. They're data, not building heights.

9
00:01:02,230 --> 00:01:04,840
Chapter two: one budget for the whole chain.

10
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:12,370
The portfolio optimizer tests every hotel's options and funds the best mix within one budget.

11
00:01:12,470 --> 00:01:19,820
Every dollar on this bar is a package the engine evaluated. Placeholder costs are named, and it's all labeled as a projection.

12
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:28,170
You can also ask the AI about the whole chain. It asks the engine to rank the hotels or split a budget.

13
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:32,670
Chapter three: fly into the building.

14
00:01:32,770 --> 00:01:37,480
Here's the hotel's digital twin: the building, its energy and its costs.

15
00:01:37,580 --> 00:02:02,180
The sidebar gives a short briefing and the top opportunities, each with one next step.

16
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:10,120
Hotel health compares it with ENERGY STAR and national survey data, as context, not a rating.

17
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:20,470
Each zone is colored by what it costs.

18
00:02:20,570 --> 00:02:29,520
It's a living building. On a January night, the heating runs and lit windows are guests in their rooms…

19
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:54,050
…and on a July afternoon, cooling peaks. The hourly readout follows the model.

20
00:02:54,150 --> 00:03:22,040
Press play, and the whole year runs: night and morning, winter and summer.

21
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:33,650
Explode the floors to see inside: lit rooms, rooms set back by smart controls, and the plant.

22
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:38,440
Room by room, you can see who is in, who is out, and what each floor uses.

23
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:43,860
Correct the equipment the hotel actually has, and the baseline follows. A correction is never counted as a saving.

24
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:47,910
The flow view follows the money from the grid and the gas line to every zone and use.

25
00:03:48,010 --> 00:03:57,370
Switch it to carbon: gas is cheap per unit but carbon-heavy, so heating and hot water grow.

26
00:03:57,470 --> 00:04:06,010
Stress tests show what a heat wave, a cold snap or a price rise would add to the bill.

27
00:04:06,110 --> 00:04:15,570
Chapter four: ask the AI to test a decision.

28
00:04:15,670 --> 00:04:22,130
Ask in plain English, by typing or with the mic.

29
00:04:22,230 --> 00:04:26,740
The AI doesn't guess. It asks the engine to run the what-if, and every number it quotes is checked against the model.

30
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:39,720
One click puts the result on the twin.

31
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:44,400
Now test a fix: smart room controls set back empty rooms automatically. See what that saves before buying anything.

32
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:51,620
Chapter five: what should she buy?

33
00:04:51,720 --> 00:05:03,360
The optimizer tests every upgrade combination within the budget, with comfort limits and confirmed incentives counted.

34
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:11,610
Here's the top package, with its cost, yearly savings, payback, ten-year value and carbon.

35
00:05:11,710 --> 00:05:20,670
The ten-year projection shows what you pay, what it saves as prices rise, and when it pays back.

36
00:05:20,770 --> 00:05:29,300
Why not solar? On the same assumptions, it pays back far more slowly here.

37
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:38,980
Or build your own plan, and compare it side by side with the recommendation.

38
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:43,250
Apply the package, and the twin and the KPI strip show before and after.

39
00:05:43,350 --> 00:05:45,830
Chapter six: hand it to the owner.

40
00:05:45,930 --> 00:06:00,350
One click creates a PDF report for the owner and the bank, with every assumption listed and every estimate labeled.

41
00:06:00,450 --> 00:06:08,830
Every key figure explains how it was calculated, and where the data comes from.

42
00:06:08,930 --> 00:06:19,210
And loading twelve months of utility bills calibrates the model to the real building.

43
00:06:19,310 --> 00:06:23,220
Back to the chain: the map is right where she left it.

44
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:28,320
Energy Twin. Simulate smarter. Spend better. Save energy.

